COMPUTER NETWORK= A network is essentially a system of interconnected elements—whether wires, computers, people, or organizations—that communicate or work together. In computing, it means devices linked to share data and resources, while in everyday life it can mean social or professional connection

2.TYPES OF NETWORK 

Type                                          Scope                                                  Example
LAN (Local Area Network)             Small, localized (home, office)                     Sharing files between PCs in an office 
WAN (Wide Area Network)              Large, across cities/countries                      The internet itself 
MAN (Metropolitan Area Network)      City-wide                                           Cable TV distribution 
Wireless Network (Wi-Fi)             Wireless connections                                Home Wi-Fi router 
Peer-to-Peer Network                 Devices connect directly                            File sharing between two laptops 

3.CLIENT VS SERVER

CLIENT 

Definition: The end-user’s device or application 

Role: Sends requests to servers and displays results.

Examples: Your browser when you type google.com.

A mobile app fetching data from an online API.

Technologies: HTML, CSS, JavaScript, React, Angular, Vue.

Responsibilities: Render the user interface (UI).

Handle user interactions (clicks, typing).

Perform local logic (form validation, animations).

Request data from servers via APIs.

SERVER

Definition: A remote computer/system that stores data and runs the core logic of applications.

Role: Processes client requests, manages data, and sends back responses.

Examples: A web server hosting Facebook.

A database server storing user accounts.

Technologies: Python, Java, PHP, Node.js, .NET, databases (MySQL, MongoDB).

Responsibilities: Handle authentication & authorization.

Process and store data in databases.

Execute business logic (e.g., calculating prices).

Generate dynamic content for clients.


