5. ROUTER,SWITCH,MODEM,ACESS POINT

ROUTER:-

Purpose: Connects different networks together (e.g., your home network to the internet).

Function: Routes data packets between networks using IP addresses.

Example: The box your ISP gives you that connects your home devices to the internet.

Key Point: Think of it as a traffic director deciding the best path for data.


SWITCH:-

Purpose: Connects multiple devices within the same local network (LAN).

Function: Forwards data only to the specific device it’s meant for, using MAC addresses.

Example: Office switch connecting PCs, printers, and servers.

Key Point: Like a post office clerk who delivers letters only to the right desk.


MODEM:-

Purpose: Converts signals between your ISP’s line (telephone/cable/fiber) and your home network.

Function: Modulates and demodulates signals (digital ↔ analog).

Example: DSL modem for telephone lines, cable modem for coaxial cable.

Key Point: Acts as a translator between your ISP and your router.


ACESS PONT (AP):-

Purpose: Provides wireless connectivity (Wi-Fi) to devices.

Function: Extends a wired network into a wireless one.

Example: Wi-Fi hotspot in a café or your home router’s Wi-Fi feature.

Key Point: Like a wireless doorway into the network.
